ishop on d5.","The next best move, 29.f3, would drop White's winning chances from 50% to 27% (−2.71).","29.Bxd5 stops the mate threat …Qh1#.","Black was threatening …Qh1#, with mate in 1. Bxd5 takes care of it.","Trades bishops.","The bishop on d5 can be taken by the queen on e4."]},{"ply":72,"name":"36…d5","cls":"great","color":"b","headline":"36…d5! The only good move here: every alternative is clearly worse.","points":["A pawn break: it challenges the c4 pawn to open lines.","Shores up the e4 pawn, which was under as much attack as it had defence (1 against 1).","The next best move, 36…Ra8, would drop Black's winning chances from 62% to 50% (−0.05).","36…d5 wins a pawn, picking it up with 37…bxa4, and stops Rc2.","Sets up a sequence and wins a pawn once the captures settle, picking it up with 37…bxa4.","White was threatening Rc2, winning a pawn, picking it up with 37.cxb5. d5 takes care of it."]},{"ply":80,"name":"40…Ra8","cls":"miss","color":"b","headline":"40…Ra8 misses the chance 40.Qf6 gave: 40…Re8 would have punished it. Black's winning chances go from 87% to 76%.","points":["White answers 41.Qb2, which protects the a3 pawn, which was hanging.","Better was 40…Re8 (−5.18)."],"better":"40…Re8 wins a pawn, picking it up with 41…Qxf5. After 41.f5 Qxf5 42.Qg5 Ra8 43.Ra1 Kg7, Black comes out a pawn up (−5.18)."},{"ply":106,"name":"53…e3","cls":"miss","color":"b","headline":"53…e3 lets the win slip: 53…Qe5 kept Black winning. Black's winning chances go from 96% to 67%.","points":["White answers 54.Qd4+: the queen forks the king and the pawn: both can't be saved at once.","The pawn on e3 can be taken by the f2 pawn.","After Qd4+, White wins 1 point of material within 4 moves.","Better was 53…Qe5 (−8.76): shores up the c7 pawn, which was under as much attack as it had defence (1 against 1)."],"better":"53…Qe5 shores up the c7 pawn. After 54.Rd1 e3 55.Kh2 e2 56.Rxd2 Rxd2 57.Qxd2 e1=Q 58.Qxe1 Qxe1 59.Kg2, the rooks are traded, the pawn promotes on e1, the queens come off and Black comes out 7 points of material up (−8.76)."},{"ply":111,"name":"56.Qe7+","cls":"blunder","color":"w","headline":"56.Qe7+ is a blunder: White's winning chances go from 50% to 20%.","points":["Black answers 56…Kg8: the king steps out of check to g8.","Better was 56.Rd1 (0.00): the rook takes the half-open d-file, aiming at the d2 pawn."],"better":"56.Rd1 takes the half-open d-file, aiming at the d2 pawn, and adds pressure on the d2 pawn. Engine line: 56…Kf7 57.f3 c6 58.Kf2 Ra2 59.Ke2 (0.00)."},{"ply":112,"name":"56…Kg6","cls":"miss","color":"b","headline":"56…Kg6 misses the chance 56.Qe7+ gave: 56…Kg8 would have punished it. Black's winning chances go from 80% to 66%.","points":["White answers 57.Qe8+, which gives check, so the reply is forced.","Better was 56…Kg8 (−3.69): the king steps out of check to g8."],"better":"56…Kg8 prepares …Kg7 and …Kg6 and steps the king out of check to g8. After 57.Qe8+ Kg7 58.Qe7+ Kg6 59.Qe8+ Kf5 60.Qxh5+ Ke4, Black is a pawn down (−3.69)."}],"opening":{"name":"Trompowsky Attack","eco":"A45","points":["Both sides followed opening theory up to 4…b6: 8 moves in all, 4 by White and 4 by Black.","Theory runs out after 4…b6: too few games between strong players reached this position to go further."]}}